## Proof

Let $M$ and $N$ be the midpoints of the diagonals $AC$ and $BD$, respectively. We prove $M=N$, which is equivalent to $ABCD$ being a parallelogram.

Place the origin at $M$. Then $A=-\mathbf{x}$ and $C=\mathbf{x}$ for some vector $\mathbf{x}$ with $|\mathbf{x}|=\tfrac12 AC>0$. Write $B=\mathbf{m}+\mathbf{y}$ and $D=\mathbf{m}-\mathbf{y}$, where $\mathbf{m}=\overrightarrow{MN}$ and $|\mathbf{y}|=\tfrac12 BD>0$. Then $M=N$ (equivalently, $ABCD$ is a parallelogram) iff $\mathbf{m}=\mathbf{0}$.

Set $\mathbf{s}=\mathbf{x}+\mathbf{y}$ and $\mathbf{t}=\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{y}$. A direct computation gives the side lengths
$$AB=|\mathbf{s}+\mathbf{m}|,\quad CD=|\mathbf{s}-\mathbf{m}|,\quad BC=|\mathbf{t}-\mathbf{m}|,\quad DA=|\mathbf{t}+\mathbf{m}|,$$
and the diagonals are $AC=2|\mathbf{x}|$, $BD=2|\mathbf{y}|$.

The two given conditions become
$$|\mathbf{s}+\mathbf{m}|+|\mathbf{s}-\mathbf{m}|=2\sqrt2\,|\mathbf{x}|, \tag{I}$$
$$|\mathbf{t}+\mathbf{m}|+|\mathbf{t}-\mathbf{m}|=2\sqrt2\,|\mathbf{y}|. \tag{II}$$

By the triangle inequality,
$$|\mathbf{s}+\mathbf{m}|+|\mathbf{s}-\mathbf{m}|\ge|(\mathbf{s}+\mathbf{m})+(\mathbf{s}-\mathbf{m})|=2|\mathbf{s}|=2|\mathbf{x}+\mathbf{y}|,$$
so from (I) we get $|\mathbf{x}+\mathbf{y}|\le\sqrt2\,|\mathbf{x}|$, i.e.
$$|\mathbf{x}+\mathbf{y}|^{2}\le 2|\mathbf{x}|^{2}. \tag{A}$$
Similarly, from (II),
$$|\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{y}|^{2}\le 2|\mathbf{y}|^{2}. \tag{B}$$

Adding (A) and (B):
$$|\mathbf{x}+\mathbf{y}|^{2}+|\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{y}|^{2}\le 2|\mathbf{x}|^{2}+2|\mathbf{y}|^{2}.$$
But the parallelogram identity gives $|\mathbf{x}+\mathbf{y}|^{2}+|\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{y}|^{2}=2|\mathbf{x}|^{2}+2|\mathbf{y}|^{2}$, so equality holds throughout. Hence both (A) and (B) are equalities.

Equality in (A) is equality in the triangle inequality $|\mathbf{s}+\mathbf{m}|+|\mathbf{s}-\mathbf{m}|\ge 2|\mathbf{s}|$, which holds iff $\mathbf{s}+\mathbf{m}$ and $\mathbf{s}-\mathbf{m}$ are non‑negatively parallel. Writing $\mathbf{s}+\mathbf{m}=\lambda(\mathbf{s}-\mathbf{m})$ with $\lambda\ge 0$ gives $(1-\lambda)\mathbf{s}+(1+\lambda)\mathbf{m}=\mathbf{0}$; if $\lambda=1$ then $\mathbf{m}=\mathbf{0}$, and otherwise $\mathbf{m}=\frac{\lambda-1}{\lambda+1}\mathbf{s}$. In every case $\mathbf{m}\parallel\mathbf{s}=\mathbf{x}+\mathbf{y}$. By the same argument applied to (B), $\mathbf{m}\parallel\mathbf{t}=\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{y}$.

Since $ABCD$ is convex, its diagonals $AC$ and $BD$ intersect in the interior, so they are not parallel; hence $\mathbf{x}$ and $\mathbf{y}$ are not parallel. Therefore $\mathbf{s}=\mathbf{x}+\mathbf{y}$ and $\mathbf{t}=\mathbf{x}-\mathbf{y}$ are not parallel (indeed $\mathbf{s}\parallel\mathbf{t}$ would force $\mathbf{x}\parallel\mathbf{y}$).

Now $\mathbf{m}$ is parallel to both $\mathbf{s}$ and $\mathbf{t}$, which are non‑parallel directions; the only such vector is $\mathbf{m}=\mathbf{0}$. Thus $M=N$: the diagonals of $ABCD$ bisect each other, so $ABCD$ is a parallelogram. $\square$

